Here’s a clear and detailed explanation of the difference between an interface and an abstract class in Java, with examples, suitable for interviews:

1. Definition

Interface:

A contract that defines what a class must do, without specifying how (mostly).

Can contain abstract methods, default methods (with implementation), static methods, and private methods (Java 8+).

Abstract Class:

A class that cannot be instantiated and can contain both abstract methods (no implementation) and concrete methods (with implementation).

Used when classes share common behavior but some methods need to be implemented by subclasses.

2. Key Differences
Feature	Interface	Abstract Class
Methods	By default abstract (except default/static methods)	Can have abstract and concrete methods
Fields	Can have public static final constants only	Can have instance variables (fields) with any access modifier
Multiple Inheritance	Supports multiple interfaces	Java doesn’t support multiple abstract class inheritance
Constructor	No constructors	Can have constructors
Access Modifiers	Methods are public by default; fields are public static final	Can have private, protected, public methods and fields
Implementation	Classes must implement interface methods	Subclasses may override abstract methods; concrete methods can be inherited
3. When to Use

Use an Interface when:

You want to define a contract for unrelated classes.

You need multiple inheritance.

You want all methods to be implemented by subclasses (no state needed).

Use an Abstract Class when:

You have shared behavior/state among related classes.

You want to provide default implementations that subclasses can reuse.

You want to define some methods as abstract but also have concrete helper methods.

4. Example

Interface Example:

interface Vehicle {
    void start();
    void stop();
}

class Car implements Vehicle {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }
    public void stop() {
        System.out.println("Car stopped");
    }
}


Abstract Class Example:

abstract class Vehicle {
    String brand;

    Vehicle(String brand) {
        this.brand = brand;
    }

    abstract void start();

    void honk() {
        System.out.println("Beep beep!");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    Car(String brand) {
        super(brand);
    }

    void start() {
        System.out.println(brand + " car started");
    }
}

